content:
Defining a form's shape once as a Zod schema and deriving both the TypeScript type and the runtime validation from it removes an entire category of bugs where the type and the validation logic drift apart.

```ts
const postSchema = z.object({
  title: z.string().min(3).max(120),
  content: z.string().min(50).max(20000),
  tags: z.array(z.string()).max(5).optional(),
});
type PostInput = z.infer<typeof postSchema>;
```

Wired into `react-hook-form` via `@hookform/resolvers/zod`, the same schema drives field-level error messages in the UI. The important part for this project: the exact same schema runs again on the server inside the Route Handler before writing to Firestore, so a request that bypasses the UI — a script, a malicious client — can't skip validation. Client-side validation is for UX; server-side validation is for correctness.
